<TopicPage slug="subjects/neuroscience/expert/consciousness-hard-problem">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Consciousness is neuroscience's ultimate question: <strong>why is there subjective
    experience at all?</strong> The easy problems (which circuits do what) are being solved; the
    hard problem (Chalmers) — why information processing feels like something — remains open.
    The field's modern shape is two competing theories forced into adversarial collaboration,
    with real clinical stakes underneath.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the hard problem, the two leading theories, and the experiment that pitted them" widget="equationStep" config={conscCfg} />

  <h2>Approaches and their critics</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Neural correlates of consciousness (NCC):</strong> the minimal brain activity sufficient for a specific conscious percept — binocular rivalry experiments isolate it (same input, changing experience). Finding correlates is step one; explaining them is step two.</li>
    <li><strong>Anesthesia as the off-switch:</strong> consciousness vanishes reliably under anaesthetics; studying the transition (activity collapses, connectivity fragments) constrains every theory. Anaesthesiology is experimental philosophy of mind with mortality stakes.</li>
    <li><strong>Panpsychism and illusionism:</strong> the philosophical flanks — maybe experience is fundamental to matter (Goff), or maybe "experience" is an introspective illusion (Frankish, Graziano). Both camps take the data seriously and the intuitions differently.</li>
    <li><strong>Why humility is the right posture:</strong> a field where two theories disagree on predictions, experiments are running, and clinical tools depend on the answers — consciousness research is science at its most honest frontier.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Chalmers (1995); Baars; Tononi; Dehaene, Consciousness and the Brain (2014); Cogitate Consortium, Nature (2023); Owen et al., Science (2006).</p>
</TopicPage>
